WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:06.039
The mist was even heavier when I reached the marshes, carrying the things I'd stolen for the

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:13.205
convict. Gates and cows seemed to burst out from the fog and cry, "A boy with

3
00:00:13.206 --> 00:00:18.197
somebody else's pork pie! Stop him!" I'd just crossed a ditch when I spotted the convict. He

4
00:00:18.198 --> 00:00:21.189
was asleep. I went forward quietly and touched him on the shoulder. He instantly jumped up,

5
00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:25.261
and I saw that it was not the same man! Yes, he was dressed all in gray

6
00:00:25.262 --> 00:00:30.602
and had a great iron on his leg, but his face was different. He swore and

7
00:00:30.603 --> 00:00:37.190
tried to hit me. Then he ran off into the mist, stumbling as he went, and I

8
00:00:37.191 --> 00:00:43.682
lost him. "It's the young man!" I thought, remembering how the first convict had told me

9
00:00:43.683 --> 00:00:48.261
about a dangerous, younger companion. My heart full of fear, I ran on and soon found the

10
00:00:48.262 --> 00:00:53.586
right man. He didn't turn me upside down this time, but left me right-side up while

11
00:00:53.587 --> 00:01:00.999
I opened my bundle. Then he was gobbling bread, cheese, and pork pie all at once, looking

12
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.832
distrustfully into the mist every few minutes. "You brought no one with you?" he asked. "No,

13
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:13.332
sir." "Well, I believe you." The man took sharp, sudden bites, just like a dog. "I'm glad

14
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:15.824
you're enjoying it," I said boldly. "Thank you, my boy. I am." "But I'm afraid you

15
00:01:15.825 --> 00:01:21.975
won't leave any food for him," I added. "And there's no more where that came from." "Him?

16
00:01:21.976 --> 00:01:24.483
Who's him?" "The young man who was hiding with you." The man laughed. "He doesn't want

17
00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:26.824
any food." "I thought he looked as if he did." The man stopped eating and regarded me

18
00:01:26.825 --> 00:01:30.816
with surprise. "You saw him? When?" "Just now when I found him and thought it was

19
00:01:30.817 --> 00:01:33.300
you." He grabbed me by the collar. "Show me which way he went, and I'll hunt him

20
00:01:33.301 --> 00:01:38.713
down like a bloodhound. Give me that file, boy!" I pointed in the direction that the

21
00:01:38.714 --> 00:01:41.467
young man had gone, but the convict was down on the wet grass, filing away at his

22
00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:49.292
leg iron. I told him I must go. He took no notice so I slipped away.

23
00:01:49.293 --> 00:01:56.038
When I reached home, I expected to find a constable in the kitchen, waiting to arrest

24
00:01:56.039 --> 00:02:00.467
me. But there was no constable, and no one had even discovered my theft. My sister was

25
00:02:00.468 --> 00:02:05.451
busy getting the house ready for the festivities of the day. Joe crossed two fingers and secretly

26
00:02:05.452 --> 00:02:09.443
showed them to me—our sign that Mrs. Joe was in a bad temper. Mr. Wopsle, the

27
00:02:09.444 --> 00:02:13.276
church clerk, was dining with us as well as Mr. Hubble, a wheel maker, and his wife.

28
00:02:13.277 --> 00:02:19.768
Also invited was Mr. Pumblechook, Joe's uncle, who was a wealthy corn merchant in a nearby

29
00:02:19.769 --> 00:02:25.355
town. The dinner hour came, and the company came, and still there was no word about the

30
00:02:25.356 --> 00:02:31.173
food I'd stolen. Mr. Wopsle said grace, ending with the hope that we should be truly

31
00:02:31.174 --> 00:02:36.736
grateful. My sister stared at me and said in a low, reproachful voice, "Do you hear that?

32
00:02:36.737 --> 00:02:41.157
Be grateful." Mr. Pumblechook added, "Especially be grateful, boy, to those who brought you up." Mrs.

33
00:02:41.158 --> 00:02:47.569
Hubble wondered why the young were never grateful. Her husband solved the mystery by saying, "They're naturally

34
00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:52.728
vicious." Everybody then murmured, "True!" and looked at me in a particularly unpleasant manner. Joe always

35
00:02:52.729 --> 00:02:55.799
comforted me when he could. He often did this at dinner by giving me gravy. Since there

36
00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.299
was plenty of gravy today, Joe spooned about half a pint onto my plate. All through

37
00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:06.299
dinner I clutched the table leg in fear that my theft would be discovered. Little by

38
00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:11.886
little I became calm enough to release my grasp and eat some pudding. I began to think

39
00:03:11.887 --> 00:03:17.124
I should survive the day, when my sister announced, "To finish the meal, you must taste a

40
00:03:17.125 --> 00:03:21.807
delicious pork pie!" I clutched the table leg immediately. My sister went out to the pantry

41
00:03:21.808 --> 00:03:26.291
to get the pie, and I heard Joe say, "You shall have some, Pip." I have never

42
00:03:26.292 --> 00:03:34.370
been certain whether I uttered a yell of terror out loud or merely in my mind.

43
00:03:34.371 --> 00:03:42.855
But I felt I could bear no more. I released the table leg and ran for my

44
00:03:42.856 --> 00:03:48.109
life. As I opened the door, I ran headfirst into a group of soldiers with muskets.

45
00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:53.768
A sergeant held out a pair of handcuffs to me, saying, "Here you are. Come on!" The

46
00:03:53.769 --> 00:03:58.601
appearance of soldiers on our doorstep caused everyone to rise from the table in confusion. Mrs.

47
00:03:58.602 --> 00:04:04.007
Joe, re-entering the room empty-handed, started to say something about the missing pie. But seeing the soldiers,

48
00:04:04.008 --> 00:04:09.427
she stopped and stared. "Excuse me, ladies and gentlemen," said the sergeant. "I'm on a chase

49
00:04:09.428 --> 00:04:17.919
in the name of the king, and I need the blacksmith. These handcuffs are broken." Joe examined

50
00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:26.903
the handcuffs, and I began to realize they were not for me. "We're after two convicts,"

51
00:04:26.904 --> 00:04:31.244
explained the sergeant as Joe took the handcuffs out to his forge. "The men are thought to

52
00:04:31.245 --> 00:04:36.744
be on the marshes. Has anyone here seen them?" Everybody except me said no. Nobody thought

53
00:04:36.745 --> 00:04:40.911
to ask me. Everyone had forgotten about the pie. When Joe's job was done, he mustered the

54
00:04:40.912 --> 00:04:47.974
courage to propose that some of us go out with the soldiers. Mr. Pumblechook and Mr.

55
00:04:47.975 --> 00:04:54.149
Hubble declined. But Mr. Wopsle, Joe, and I were permitted to march behind the soldiers. As

56
00:04:54.150 --> 00:05:00.799
we entered the marshes, it began to sleet, so Joe put me on his back and carried

57
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.950
me. Now that we were out in this dismal place, I began to wonder if my convict

58
00:05:05.951 --> 00:05:12.799
would think that I was the one who had brought the soldiers. Suddenly we heard shouting.

59
00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:20.791
The soldiers ran like deer and so did Joe. We soon came upon the two convicts fighting.

60
00:05:20.792 --> 00:05:26.696
"I captured him for you!" said my convict, the one I'd met first. "Here he is!"

61
00:05:26.697 --> 00:05:32.530
"He tried to murder me," said the young convict. "I would have been a dead man if

62
00:05:32.531 --> 00:05:42.180
you had not shown up." "He lies!" said my convict with fierce energy. "Do you see

63
00:05:42.181 --> 00:05:45.085
how his eyes wander and will not meet mine? That's how he looked when we were on

64
00:05:45.086 --> 00:05:49.426
trial together." "Enough!" said the sergeant. He ordered a soldier to light the torches, and my

65
00:05:49.427 --> 00:05:55.323
convict caught sight of me. I tried to signal that I had not betrayed him. He stared

66
00:05:55.324 --> 00:06:02.585
at me, but I didn't know if he understood. We marched to the river. Suddenly my

67
00:06:02.586 --> 00:06:05.744
convict spoke up. "I wish to say something regarding this escape. It may keep someone else from

68
00:06:05.745 --> 00:06:09.252
being under suspicion. I was starving and I took some food from the blacksmith's." "We don't

69
00:06:09.253 --> 00:06:15.069
know what you've done," said Joe, "but we wouldn't have you starve to death. Would we, Pip?"

70
00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:21.491
My convict turned away. By the light of the torches, we saw the black prison ship

71
00:06:21.492 --> 00:06:24.983
lying offshore. My convict was rowed out to the ship and taken on board. Then the torches

72
00:06:24.984 --> 00:06:30.801
were flung into the water and went out, as if it were all over for him.

